ical), but memory for numbers weak (attention), nasty remarks (crew cooperation). Candidate D: responsibility, concentration, problem solving, but hot temper (stress resilience), know-it-all (cooperation). Candidate A: quick correct decisions, calm, technical, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Candidate C: anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nI think Candidate B's interpersonal issue is a serious red flag for crew safety. Candidate D's hot temper also serious. Candidate A's communication difficulty may be mitigated? But egocentric and unwillingness to learn is also concerning. Candidate C's not open to new ideas may affect adaptability.\n\nMaybe the best is Candidate C: strong technical and leadership, anticipates danger, but attitude issues. However, show-off may affect crew cooperation, but perhaps less dangerous than nasty remarks or hot temper. Also not open to new ideas could affect adaptability.\n\nGiven limited info, I might lean toward Candidate C as best overall technical competence and decision quality, with leadership, but need to address attitude. I will recommend Candidate C, noting concerns but overall fit.\n\nThus public message: state recommendation for Candidate C, reference that we haven't discussed others yet but based on my notes, C's anticipation and leadership are strong, while concerns about show-off and openness are noted but perhaps manageable.\n\nProvide evidence: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nOk.\n\nNow produce output."}
